{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>And he asked about their welfare.</b> Meaning, first he asked about their own welfare, for it is proper manners to precede by inquiring after the welfare of those who are present, and this is what it says <b>about them, about their welfare</b>, and only afterward did he ask about the one who was not there: ״הֲשָׁלוֹם אֲבִיכֶם״ etc." "<b>Is there peace for your aged father</b> etc. It is difficult: how is it justified to inquire about peace before inquiring ״Is he still alive?״ And if the intent of the inquiry about peace is that which he explained in saying ״Is he still alive,״ why did he repeat it? It is also difficult in the brothers' reply, for they said ״Peace etc. he is still alive״ — after they said ״peace,״ what is the novelty in their saying ״he is still alive״?" "Or perhaps he intended to say: if he had his strength then, is his strength now the same or has it weakened somewhat due to old age, and this is what he was precise in saying <b>is he still</b> — meaning, as he was in the former time. And therefore he was also clever to arrange the inquiry about peace before the inquiry ״is he still alive,״ to convey his intent as we have explained. Accordingly, the inquiry ״is he still alive״ is not subsumed under the inquiry about his peace, for even regarding a man who has grown old and some of his faculties have naturally diminished, one may say of him: peace is upon him."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 43, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:48:25.310269299Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}